00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:03.350 align:start position:0%
 
coat of arms family crest  heraldry what do these names mean and  what are the differences  the image you see before you is not a  coat of arms  nor a family crest okay okay i hear you  say  so what is it what are coats of arms and  what are family crests  and where do these things come from we  see them all over but what do they mean  all good questions and i'm here to  answer them first of all  this entire image is called a heraldic  achievement the term code of arms only  refers to the image  within the shield the reason it's called  a coat of arms is because the design  would often be worn on an actual coat  called a tabard  next up family crest often people will  call this entire image a crest  but it's not the crest is only the small  image atop the helmet you see here  heraldry  is the use of designs typically on  shields  that represent a person family or  organization  heraldry proper where specific people or  families had a unique heraldic design  didn't start until the high middle ages  around the 12th century that said  designs on shields go back to classical  antiquity  conventional wisdom states that this  practice started as a way of telling  soldiers apart during a battle so that  warriors on the same side of a conflict  wouldn't kill each other this  seems to make a lot of sense and it may  well be true in some cases  but a lot of scholars dispute it as it  has little to no evidence to back it up  the designs would have been there for  other reasons  like decoration a way to strike fear in  an opponent  or as a show status besides much of the  time  soldiers in the medieval period had to  acquire their own supplies  so uniformity was uncommon anyway the  practice became popular  and eventually certain individuals  became known for having  specific designs on their shields  richard the lion heart for instance was  known to have  lions on his shield as did geoffrey  plantagenet  the count of anju with the rising  popularity of these designs  knights and noblemen started to adopt  the practice and inevitably argue over  who had the right to specific designs  this caused the powers that be  specifically the kings of europe to  develop governing bodies that tracked  and handed out these designs  so as to keep them unique with this the  title of herald was created  a position tasked with knowing the ranks  and achievements of the knights that  would come to the castle  as well as design and award heraldic  achievements this practice developed  into the college of arms  which still exists to this day in  britain the achievement is composed of  eight parts  the shield the helm the torse the crest  the supporters the compartment and the  motto the shield contains the field  and the images depicted on the field are  called charges  the term tincture refers to the colors  used  in the design the names of these  tinctures as well as the language  surrounding all of heraldry  is a kind of anglicized norman french  traditionally  there are seven main tinctures two  metals  or gold and argent silver then  there are five colors ghouls red azure  blue vert green sable black  and pure purple furs are tinctures meant  to resemble  animal furs worn by noblemen there are  two main furs  hermann which mimics the fur of a weasel  and  there which mimics the fur of a squirrel  over time  three more colors were added these were  known as stains  sanguine blood red murray mulberry and  tane a sort of burnt orange lastly there  are some non-traditional tinctures that  have developed in the 20th century  the most common being blue celeste a sky  blue  carnation a caucasian flesh tone rose  and sandre a grey the rule of tinctures  is the most important part of heraldic  design  it is simple stating that metals shall  not be placed atop  metals and colors shall not be atop  colors so a design like this would not  be allowed  but a design like that would there are  exceptions to the rule  for instance when the shield is divided  the tinctures are seen as  beside one another not atop also when a  charge is depicted as  proper meaning its natural color the  rule does not apply there are also  certain historical exceptions where the  rule was simply not  followed like for the kingdom of  jerusalem also  stains in this case are seen as colors  and furs don't come into the equation at  all also  interestingly blue celeste is seen as a  metal presumably because of its light  color the field itself can be divided a  number of ways  with a variety of dividing lines it can  be divided in two  or three or more there are also what are  called variations of the field  like so now let's move to the charges to  reiterate  charges are images put on the field of  the shield  to start off there are the ordinaries  these are the most common charges and  they consist mostly of geometric shapes  and are similar to divisions and  variations other than the ordinaries  common charges include crosses animals  flowers body parts celestial bodies and  objects  charges can have multiple meanings a  tree in one achievement might mean  strength while on another it may signify  a specific tree in a certain place  some common meanings for common charges  do  exist however lines tend to represent  courage and royalty  crosses of course represent christian  faith books are often  found on the arms of schools and  universities as it represents knowledge  keys are found on the arms of clergymen  as they hold the keys to the church  to properly describe an achievement is  to blaze in it  the blazon is a kind of insider  shorthand to quickly describe the design  on the shield  for instance the blazon of my own family  coat of arms is as follows  azure two bars ore in chief across  formefiche of the last azure means the  field is blue  two bars describes these ordinaries or  means they are gold chief is the term of  the top middle position of the field  and it is a cross the terms forme and  fije are more heraldry speak  describing its shape and of the last  means  the cross is the same color of the last  charge  mentioned which were the gold bars so  the cross is also gold  so now that we're finally down with a  shield what about the rest let's start  with the supporters  the supporters are the figures on either  side of the shield holding up or  supporting the shield  generally they are animals but sometimes  you'll see human figures or even  buildings used  certain animals have connections to  certain nations most famously  lions stand for england and unicorns for  scotland  supporters stand on the compartment  usually  a small landscape it's important to  point out that supporters are not seen  on every achievement  in fact they're not seen on most  achievements and are only given to those  of high status  or as an award for a special service the  helm is set atop the shield  traditionally the type of helm didn't  really mean anything but as heraldry  developed different styles of helm  started to symbolize different ranks  for instance an open visored helm means  nobility or royalty  a closed visor means lower nobility or  gentry women and clergymen generally do  not have helms on their achievements  as they would not go to war the taurus  is a circle of cloth above the helm  royalty and nobility will generally have  a crown or coronet instead above the  taurus is the crest  crests came from the practice of knights  having fanciful objects atop their helms  usually for tournaments like charges  they can represent different things  crests were sometimes used as badges  which gave rise to the misnomer of  crests referring to the entire  achievement  mantling is the cloth around the helm  again this comes from the way knights  would be  armored and dressed it is generally  simply decorative lastly is the motto  a motto would not be given as much as  chosen by the person or family receiving  the arms  often it would be added years or  generations later  also mottos were usually in latin so now  that you know the different parts of the  achievement  you may want to go and find your own  family coat of arms if you have one  or design your own well a couple of  words of warning on how to spot bogus  arms  that will also help you design genuine  looking arms  first off official achievements are  given out by official  organizations if you live in a country  with one of those  making unofficial arms might be seen as  disrespectful or ill-advised  you may be able to petition for official  arms if so desired  moving on to spotting fake achievements  first if the rules of  tinctures are not followed it probably  isn't official  second crowns or other royal charges  used when clearly not deserved  is a red flag third thin crosses or  other thin  incorrect divisions fourth supporters  not in a compartment  and last use of cliche charges like  lions or fleur-de-lis  obviously these are not hard and fast  rules especially with the cliche charges  they are cliche for a reason after all  they all have exceptions  but if you see multiple red flags  understand the achievement probably  isn't official  now go forth with understanding and  knowledge of this small but very cool  aspect of european history  thanks for watching  you

